{"timestamp":"2026-10-29T17:44:26.705Z","ordinal":494675,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"agent_message","id":"amsg_01a188c3-5b51-78f3-9be2-e3d317664406","author":"/root/plan05_readonly_review","recipient":"/root","content":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Message Type: FINAL_ANSWER\nTask name: /root\nSender: /root/plan05_readonly_review\nPayload:\n**CHANGES_REQUIRED** — exact base `fe1c91dbcb56693a34c06dd3719bea139f639833`, head `7fe2981d6e9a379ff06804b829cb9200fe7a8dbe`, diff SHA-256 `47bf8a3a8c7c1288cd77cb23a62bd1bd3abfa0ed756b17c96a7b814d765aa0e5`.\n\nThe SIGTERM/reaping change addresses my prior cancellation concern: root enumeration now runs in a killable `find` child, with SIGTERM/SIGKILL escalation, and the new fixture checks that the enumeration process is reaped.\n\n**Remaining finding:** UTF-8 validation covers only the root-enumeration stream. The later compiler scan buffers raw path bytes in [`scan-compiler-exposure.mjs`](/home/mike/nexus-code/eval/scan-compiler-exposure.mjs:175), while [`isolate.mjs`](/home/mike/nexus-code/eval/isolate.mjs:226) decodes that output with `Buffer.toString(\"utf8\")` without checking for lossy replacement. A compiler beneath a nested directory whose name contains invalid UTF-8 can therefore be decoded to a different path. If that replacement path exists but is not executable, inspection silently skips it while the actual compiler remains unmasked. The added invalid-UTF-8 regression puts the bad name directly under the root, so it exercises the validated enumeration stream and misses this case.\n\nValidate the compiler-result stream byte-for-byte before decoding, and add a nested invalid-UTF-8 path regression that proves the scan fails closed. This is a static review; I did not run tests or verify CI."}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a188b4-bcfd-7801-8430-e45b95efb101","create_time":1793295866.705413}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"user_input_order":23115}}
