{"timestamp":"2026-10-29T17:46:17.358Z","ordinal":494794,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"function_call_output","id":"fco_01a188c5-0b8e-7283-bb5f-1a54b818c882","call_id":"call_483b04038ac14fad85f63e6c304373e4","output":"{\"agents\":[{\"agent_name\":\"/root\",\"agent_status\":\"running\"},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/heldout_public_coverage_audit\",\"agent_status\":{\"completed\":\"No ownership or handoff change is visible. At `origin/main` `cbb3b653a6bb6060071d8927e08e30d63765865e`, issue #1 remains open and unassigned; its latest comment (#6483900189) reports no custodian update and no qualification runs. The Agent Mail directory shows no active project windows or designated custodian, and the public claims log has no held-out handoff record.\\n\\nThe remaining external dependency is a reachable owner identifying the designated custodian or assigning a replacement, followed by an acknowledged handoff. That custodian must handle the legacy-to-`blackbox-v1` conversion or replacement, private validation, and renewed custody acceptance before qualification. I did not contact anyone or access private corpus material.\"}},{\"agent_name\":\"/root/plan05_readonly_review\",\"agent_status\":{\"completed\":\"**CHANGES_REQUIRED** — exact base `fe1c91dbcb56693a34c06dd3719bea139f639833`, head `7fe2981d6e9a379ff06804b829cb9200fe7a8dbe`, diff SHA-256 `47bf8a3a8c7c1288cd77cb23a62bd1bd3abfa0ed756b17c96a7b814d765aa0e5`.\\n\\nThe SIGTERM/reaping change addresses my prior cancellation concern: root enumeration now runs in a killable `find` child, with SIGTERM/SIGKILL escalation, and the new fixture checks that the enumeration process is reaped.\\n\\n**Remaining finding:** UTF-8 validation covers only the root-enumeration stream. The later compiler scan buffers raw path bytes in [`scan-compiler-exposure.mjs`](/home/mike/nexus-code/eval/scan-compiler-exposure.mjs:175), while [`isolate.mjs`](/home/mike/nexus-code/eval/isolate.mjs:226) decodes that output with `Buffer.toString(\\\"utf8\\\")` without checking for lossy replacement. A compiler beneath a nested directory whose name contains invalid UTF-8 can therefore be decoded to a different path. If that replacement path exists but is not executable, inspection silently skips it while the actual compiler remains unmasked. The added invalid-UTF-8 regression puts the bad name directly under the root, so it exercises the validated enumeration stream and misses this case.\\n\\nValidate the compiler-result stream byte-for-byte before decoding, and add a nested invalid-UTF-8 path regression that proves the scan fails closed. This is a static review; I did not run tests or verify CI.\"}}]}","internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a188b4-bcfd-7801-8430-e45b95efb101","create_time":1793295977.3585613}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
